---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const siteUrl = `https://nadeemdevapps.github.io${base}`;
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Who is Nadeem Mazhar?',
    answer:
      "Nadeem Mazhar is a solo developer who runs Nadeem Dev Apps, a one-developer studio with no partners and no team. He plans, designs, and ships websites, Windows apps, Android apps, and backends, and his apps Tsumly and Kaizenly started as his own problems.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What does Nadeem build?',
    answer:
      "Nadeem builds websites, Windows apps, Android apps, and backends — local-first or cloud, whichever the project needs. His shipped apps are local-first by design, with no account, no server, and no ads, because that is the right call for a personal tool, not a limitation on what he can build.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Nadeem work with AI?',
    answer:
      "Nadeem plans the product, designs the interface, directs the build, and reviews everything before it ships, with AI tools doing the heavy lifting under his direction. The decisions are his: he explores a problem until he understands it, documents a clear plan, and ships only when the result works in daily use.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are Nadeem's strengths?",
    answer:
      "His strengths are exploration, working with AI, UI design, planning, brainstorming, and problem-solving, with security analysis on every build. He is still learning some languages and knows the basics of others, but the job, as he puts it, was never typing syntax by hand.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are Nadeem's apps free to use?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Everything Nadeem makes is free to use right now — including Kaizenly's custom sessions of 3 to 12 hours. There is nothing to pay for.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What are Tsumly, Kaizenly, and MsgLoop?',
    answer:
      "Tsumly is a local-first loan and ledger app that splits one repayment across several loans without merging them. Kaizenly is a strict focus guard for Android whose timed sessions keep distracting apps shut until the timer ends. MsgLoop is a WhatsApp message scheduler for Android — and because it automates WhatsApp, which WhatsApp's terms do not allow, bulk messaging can get a number banned.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How can I contact Nadeem?',
    answer:
      'You can reach Nadeem at nadeem.devapps@gmail.com, or find his work on GitHub at github.com/nadeemdevapps, his only social link. The contact page also has a form that opens a pre-addressed email in your own mail app.',
  },
];
const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: faq.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: faq.answer },
  })),
};
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Person',
    '@id': `${siteUrl}#nadeem`,
    name: 'Nadeem Mazhar',
    jobTitle: 'Solo Developer',
    description:
      'Solo developer. I plan first, then build with AI — phase by phase, tested. Six shipped products, built for real problems I had myself.',
    url: `${siteUrl}about/`,
    sameAs: ['https://github.com/nadeemdevapps'],
  },
  faqJsonLd,
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Nadeem Mazhar — Solo Developer"
  description="About Nadeem Mazhar — a solo developer building with AI, phase by phase, tested. Education, skills, and the plan-first way he works."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="page-top">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head reveal">
        <h1 class="display">About</h1>
      </div>
      <div class="about-prose">
        <div class="about-block reveal">
          <h3>I learn breadth-first</h3>
          <p><strong>Matriculation (Biology) — Board of Intermediate and Secondary Education (BISE) Bahawalpur — 1018/1200 (~84%)</strong></p>
          <p>Biology was chosen on purpose: a little knowledge of every field makes targeted research easy when you need the detail. I've been using a laptop since <strong>grade 3</strong>.</p>
          <p><strong>UK Level 3 Extended Diploma in IT &amp; Cyber Security — Qualifi (UK) — Superior International Colleges, Rahim Yar Khan — 3rd semester, research internship next</strong></p>
          <p>The diploma covers 18 units across the semesters, and finishes with a research-based internship.</p>
          <details class="units-toggle">
            <summary>18 units — view the list (each 10 credits)</summary>
            <ul class="units-list">
              <li><strong>EUS309</strong> — English for University Studies</li>
              <li><strong>CCSU310</strong> — Communication and Cultural Skills for University</li>
              <li><strong>BM301</strong> — An Introduction to the Business Environment</li>
              <li><strong>BM302</strong> — Business Resources</li>
              <li><strong>BM303</strong> — An Introduction to Marketing</li>
              <li><strong>BM304</strong> — Human Resource Management</li>
              <li><strong>IT301</strong> — Computer Systems</li>
              <li><strong>IT302</strong> — Coding and Website Development</li>
              <li><strong>IT303</strong> — Networks</li>
              <li><strong>IT304</strong> — Mobile Communications</li>
              <li><strong>IT305</strong> — Cyber Security</li>
              <li><strong>IT306</strong> — Social Media for Business</li>
              <li><strong>T/617/1163</strong> — Threat and Risk: Expecting the Unexpected</li>
              <li><strong>F/617/1165</strong> — Network Architecture: Communications and Protocols</li>
              <li><strong>J/617/1166</strong> — Mobile Device and Data Risks</li>
              <li><strong>R/617/1168</strong> — Investigations and Incident Response</li>
              <li><strong>R/617/1171</strong> — Solutions: Future-proofing Your Business</li>
              <li><strong>Y/617/1172</strong> — EU GDPR and Data Security</li>
            </ul>
          </details>
        </div>

        <div class="about-block reveal">
          <h3>The plan is the skill</h3>
          <p>Anyone can get AI to write code. The difference is the process: a PRD, a design doc, clear goals — then phases, built one by one, each one tested before the next starts.</p>
          <p class="steps">PRD <span class="arr">→</span> Design doc <span class="arr">→</span> Goals <span class="arr">→</span> Phases <span class="arr">→</span> Tested, one by one.</p>
          <p class="honest">I don't claim deep mastery of any one language — AI writes the code; I own the plan, the design, and the testing.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-block reveal">
          <h3>AI-first, every day</h3>
          <p>My daily toolkit spans the whole AI landscape — and <strong>OpenCode</strong> is my primary agent CLI: multi-model, and friendly to a student budget.</p>
          <div class="tools">
            <span>Muse</span>
            <span>Lovable</span>
            <span>Stitch</span>
            <span>ChatGPT</span>
            <span>Gemini</span>
            <span>Claude</span>
            <span>Kimi</span>
            <span>Minimax</span>
            <span>DeepSeek</span>
            <span>Perplexity</span>
            <span>OpenCode</span>
          </div>
          <p>I follow new AI launches closely and experiment with new tools and techniques as they land — currently studying and implementing through reverse-engineering existing products. Two I'm following right now: <a href="https://labs.google.com/pomelli/about/">Pomelli</a>, Google Labs' tool that generates on-brand content, and <a href="https://github.com/morluto/rea">REA — Reverse Engineer Anything</a>, an MCP that lets an agent inspect an app's behaviour down to the binary.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-block reveal">
          <h3>Self-taught, doubt-free</h3>
          <p>Most of what I know is self-education: research until a doubt is actually cleared — mine, and other people's too. I've been <strong>Class Representative since grade 3</strong>, which is mostly that: hearing questions and finding answers.</p>
          <p>Along the way I also picked up <strong>accounting basics</strong> — assets, liabilities, debits and credits, debtors, creditors, and the accounting equation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-block reveal">
          <h3>Why I build</h3>
          <p class="honest">I build for myself first. If a paid feature costs nothing to run — no server — it should be free.</p>
          <p>That's why my finance app exists: the features I needed were paywalled elsewhere, so I built my own — and why Kaizenly has no premium tier at all.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2>Questions, answered.</h2>
      {faqs.map((faq) => (
        <>
          <h3>{faq.question}</h3>
          <p>{faq.answer}</p>
        </>
      ))}
      <p>
        Learn more about <a href={`${base}products/tsumly/`}>Tsumly</a>, <a href={`${base}products/kaizenly/`}>Kaizenly</a>, and <a href={`${base}products/msgloop/`}>MsgLoop</a> — or <a href={`${base}contact/`}>get in touch</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
